    "# THE POWER OF PANDAS: answer a complex question in one chained expression\n",
    "# \"What is the average rush-hour speed for each day of the week?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "rush_hour_by_day = (\n",
    "    df[df['Hour'].isin([7, 8, 9, 17, 18, 19])]\n",
    "    .groupby('Day_Name')['AVERAGE_SPEED']\n",
    "    .mean()\n",
    "    .reindex(['Monday', 'Tuesday', 'Wednesday', 'Thursday', 'Friday', 'Saturday', 'Sunday'])\n",
    "    .round(1)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Rush-hour average speed by day of week (one Pandas chain!):\")\n",
    "print(rush_hour_by_day.to_string())\n",
    "print(f\"\\n>>> Worst rush-hour day: {rush_hour_by_day.idxmin()} ({rush_hour_by_day.min()} km/h)\")\n",
    "print(f\">>> Best rush-hour day:  {rush_hour_by_day.idxmax()} ({rush_hour_by_day.max()} km/h)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "part4-transition",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "> **Key Insight:** Pandas wraps NumPy arrays with labels (column names, row indices) and adds powerful I/O, datetime parsing, and missing data support. Use NumPy when you need pure speed on numeric arrays; use Pandas when you need structure and labels.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Investigation status:** Our case file is now structured — every reading has a timestamp, day name, hour, and geographic tag. Next: use Pandas' powerful **indexing** to zoom in on the bridge corridors.\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
